<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinh Kiem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1503">1503</a>–<a href="/years/1570">1570</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Trịnh Kiểm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trinh Kiem was a Vietnamese general and founder of the power of the Trinh lords. Son-in-law of the Le loyalist leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyen Kim</a>, he took command of the Later Le restoration forces after Nguyen Kim's death in 1545 and fought the Mac dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-hoa">Thanh Hoa</a>, becoming the real ruler behind the Le emperors. His rivalry with Nguyen Kim's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-hoang">Nguyen Hoang</a> began the Trinh–Nguyen division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem.jpg" alt="Trinh Kiem" width="263" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trịnh Quang Vũ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tr%E1%BB%8Bnh_Ki%E1%BB%83m_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Trịnh Kiểm, first named Trịnh Phiến, was born on 14 September 1503 in the village of Hổ in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanh-hoa">Thanh Hoa</a> province, the third of seven children of Trịnh Lâu and Hoàng Thị Ngọc Dốc. His great-grandfather Trịnh Khả had been a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-loi">Lê Lợi</a>. His father died when he was six, and the family fell into poverty; he worked as a buffalo herder and was expelled from his village with his mother after killing an injured buffalo to feed his friends.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-dang-dung">Mạc Đăng Dung</a> overthrew the Lê dynasty in 1527, Kiểm and his mother took refuge with the Mạc general Ninh Bang Hầu, who set him to tend livestock; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> horse trainer, Vũ Thì An, taught him to judge and train horses. He later fled, and the general had his mother drowned in a bamboo cage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kiểm joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyễn Kim</a>, who in 1533 placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-trang-tong">Lê Trang Tông</a> on the throne and began the war between the Southern Court of the Lê and the Northern Court of the Mạc. Kim made him commander of the cavalry, gave him his daughter Nguyễn Thị Ngọc Bảo in marriage, and in 1539 the emperor made him Grand General. After Kim was poisoned in 1545, Kiểm took command, defeated a Mạc attack at the Phù Chẩn River and was named supreme commander and Grand Mentor. From then on every important decision was his, and the emperor approved it afterwards.</p><p>From his base at Vạn Lại he recruited men of talent, held civil service examinations in Confucian studies and drew defectors such as the Mạc grand chancellor Lê Bá Ly, who came over in 1550 with more than 13,000 troops. In 1554 his armies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuan-hoa">Thuận Hóa</a> and Quảng Nam, and in 1558 he proposed that Nguyễn Hoàng govern Thuận Hóa as a frontier stronghold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In failing health, Kiểm handed power in 1569 to his eldest son, Trịnh Coi, and died in 1570. Coi was defeated by a Mạc army that year and replaced by his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-tung">Trịnh Tùng</a>, who declared himself lord and gave his father the title posthumously. Though he never won a decisive victory over the Mạc or took a higher rank than Thái Quốc Công, Kiểm laid the foundation for the Trịnh lords, who ruled Đại Việt in the name of the Lê emperors for two centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trịnh Kiểm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Real power in the north passed to the Trịnh lords, beginning with <b>Trịnh Kiểm</b>, who ruled through a parallel administration, the lord's palace (phủ chúa), controlling the army, finances and appointments, while the Lê emperors performed state rituals and conferred legitimacy, including in relations with China.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Their opponents, led by Nguyen Kim and then <b>Trinh Kiem</b>, restored a Le emperor in 1533 in Thanh Hoa, beginning the war between the Northern Mac and Southern Le courts.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-dynasty">Mac dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Trinh Kiem</b>, son-in-law of the Le loyalist leader Nguyen Kim, took command of the Le restoration forces after Nguyen Kim's death in 1545 and became the real power behind the Later Le dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinh-lords">Trinh lords</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thanh Hóa, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.81,105.78&amp;year=1546" aria-label="Thanh Hóa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7405 1430 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Trinh Kiem"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1700h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1700h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.81,105.78&amp;year=1546">What was here in 1546? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
